<article class="env-lemma" id="03H4">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/03H4">Lemma <span data-tag="03H4">66.4.3</span>.</a> Let $S$ be a scheme. Let </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix{ Z \times _ Y X \ar[r] \ar[d] &  X \ar[d] \\  Z \ar[r] &  Y }  \]
</div>
  <p> be a cartesian diagram of algebraic spaces over $S$. Then the map of sets of points </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  |Z \times _ Y X| \longrightarrow |Z| \times _{|Y|} |X|  \]
</div>
  <p> is surjective. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Namely, suppose given fields $K$, $L$ and morphisms $\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(K) \to X$, $\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(L) \to Z$, then the assumption that they agree as elements of $|Y|$ means that there is a common extension $M/K$ and $M/L$ such that $\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(M) \to \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(K) \to X \to Y$ and $\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(M) \to \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(L) \to Z \to Y$ agree. And this is exactly the condition which says you get a morphism $\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(M) \to Z \times _ Y X$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>